 abstract = {Ramp merging zones are usually identified as critical highway bottlenecks, prone to vehicular conflicts that induce severe congestion and safety risks. Conventional control approaches are often constrained by computational inefficiency and difficulty of modeling complex environments, while deep-learning-based controls still face limited interpretability. To address these challenges, this paper proposes an interpretable Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) framework, i.e., Hybrid Action Soft Actor-Critic (HASAC), for the cooperative control of Connected and Automated Vehicles (CAVs). By modifying neural networks with two independent policy heads, this integrated model produces continuous longitudinal acceleration controls and discrete lateral lane-changing decisions jointly, which significantly reduces action space dimensionality and training complexity. Additionally, the model incorporates both microscopic and macroscopic traffic states, formulating a multi-objective reward that minimizes acceleration lane queues, regulates merging gaps, and prevents myopic optimization via a global queue penalty to synergistically optimize efficiency and safety. Comparative experiments with multi-baselines demonstrate that HASAC exhibits robust superiority across varying traffic conditions. Specifically, the waiting time, merging time, number of stops, and collision risk ratio can decrease up to 96.2%, 73.9%, 81.4%, and 87.5%, respectively. The lateral-longitudinal cooperative decision-making mechanisms are systematically elucidated through spatiotemporal trajectory analysis. Meanwhile, the feature attribution analyses (based on Integrated Gradients) identify inherent effects of state features (especially speeds and locations) towards hybrid actions. Finally, validation using real-world traffic data from California confirms the effectiveness and transferability of the model, offering an applicable solution for cooperative ramp merging controls. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
 author = {Song, Li and Zong, Jiawei and Lin, Yixuan and Zhao, Xin and Lyu, Nengchao and David Fan, Wei},
